    },
    {
      "id": "QIrPnWbMCnf3dm5jasB7j",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 279,
      "y": 514,
      "width": 853.601806640625,
      "height": 240,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1419210025,
      "version": 24,
      "versionNonce": 1158908041,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1772699222531,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Let’s discuss it considering the following example:\n\nAssume, we are given an array: [2, 5, 16, 28, 139]. Here, we can apply array hashing, \nand to use that, we need to create an array of size 140. Now, what to do if we are given \na constraint that we cannot use an array whose length is greater than 10?\n\nIn order to solve this we will use the division method. We will simply consider the modulo \n10 of each element of the array(element % 10) and we will hash(pre-store and fetch) the elements \non the basis of the modulo value i.e. the remainder. The steps will look like the following:\nPre storing: hash[arr[i]%10] += 1 and Fetching: hash[number%10]\n\nNow if we apply this method to the given array, the hash array will look like the following:",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Let’s discuss it considering the following example:\n\nAssume, we are given an array: [2, 5, 16, 28, 139]. Here, we can apply array hashing, \nand to use that, we need to create an array of size 140. Now, what to do if we are given \na constraint that we cannot use an array whose length is greater than 10?\n\nIn order to solve this we will use the division method. We will simply consider the modulo \n10 of each element of the array(element % 10) and we will hash(pre-store and fetch) the elements \non the basis of the modulo value i.e. the remainder. The steps will look like the following:\nPre storing: hash[arr[i]%10] += 1 and Fetching: hash[number%10]\n\nNow if we apply this method to the given array, the hash array will look like the following:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
